===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}}
## To [[modify]] so as to create a [[difference]] or [[distinction]].
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1873|author={{w|John Earle (professor)|John Earle}}|title=The Philology of the English Tongue|edition=second|page=128
|url=https://archive.org/details/philologyofengli00earlrich/page/128/mode/2up?q=differentiated
|passage=A humble instance of Ablaut may be quoted which took place in the seventeenth century, when the word ''then'' was '''differentiated''' into the two forms ''then'' and ''than''.}}
## To [[show]] or be the [[difference]] or [[distinction]] between things.
##: {{uxi|en|What '''differentiates''' an alligator from a crocodile?}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1998|author=W. Ross Winterowd|title=The English Department: A Personal and Institutional History|passage=''Taste'' '''differentiates''' a gentleman—for instance, Mr. Addison—from a Hottentot or Laplander.|page=66|publisher=w:Southern Illinois University Press|isbn=9780809321704|url=https://www.google.co.uk/books/edition/The_English_Department/LYbETBGgjyoC?hl=en&gbpv=1&pg=PA66&printsec=frontcover&dq=differentiates}}
## To [[recognize]] as [[different]] or [[distinct]].
##: {{ant|en|conflate}}
##: {{uxi|en|How do you '''differentiate''' a fake smile from a genuine one?}}
##* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1908
|author=w:Edward Harper Parker
|title=Ancient China Simplified
|url=https://archive.org/details/ancientchinasimp00park_0/
|location=London
|publisher=w:Chapman & Hall, Ltd.
|OCLC=563512600
|page=ix
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/ancientchinasimp00park_0/page/n14/
|text=In the earlier chapters uncouth proper names are reduced to a minimum, but the Index refers by name to specific places and persons only generally mentioned in the earlier pages. For instance, the states of Lu and Chêng on pages 22 and 29 : it is hard enough to '''differentiate''' Ts‘i, Tsin, Ts‘in, and Ts‘u at the outstart, without crowding the memory with fresh names until the necessity for it absolutely arises.}}
##* {{RQ:Orwell Down and Out|passage=The mass of the rich and poor are '''differentiated''' by their incomes and nothing else, and the average millionaire is only the average dishwasher dressed in a new suit.|chapter=22}}
## {{lb|en|mathematics}} To [[calculate]] the [[derivative]] of a [[function]].
## {{lb|en|mathematics}} To calculate the [[differential]] of a function of multiple [[variable]]s.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}}
## To [[perceive]] the [[difference]] between things; to [[discriminate]].
##* {{quote-book|en|title=w:New York Times v. Sullivan| year=1964| passage=he refused to instruct that actual intent to harm or recklessness had to be found before punitive damages could be awarded, or that a verdict for respondent should '''differentiate''' between compensatory and punitive damages.}}
## {{lb|en|education}} To [[teach]] a [[lesson]] in multiple different ways in order to meet the needs of more or less advanced [[student]]s.
##* {{quote-web|en|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/local/education/teaching-for-all-levels--in-one-class/2012/05/15/gIQAv1lUSU_story.html|archiveurl=https://archive.vn/GCX37|archivedate=2023-02-11|work=w:The Washington Post|passage=Like most teaching, '''differentiating''' is a mix of art and science. When it works, "it's like a jazz rhythm," said Carol Tomlinson, an education professor at the University of Virginia and an expert on differentiation.|title=Teaching for all levels — in one class|date=2012-05-16|author=Michael Alison Chandler}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|passage=Moreover, children of STIP-teachers who showed many types of differentiation activities learnt more than children of STIP-teachers who '''differentiated''' less.|title=Supporting primary school teachers in differentiating in the regular classroom|work=Teaching and Teacher Education|year=2017|month=August|author=Tessa H.S. Eysink; Manon Hulsbeek; Hannie Gijlers|page=107116|volume=66|issn=0742-051X|doi=10.1016/j.tate.2017.04.002|publisher=w:Elsevier|location=Amsterdam}}
##* {{quote-web|en|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20220519064052/https://www.edweek.org/teaching-learning/opinion-response-ways-to-use-tech-to-differentiate-instruction/2018/06|work=w:Education Week|passage=I use technology to '''differentiate''' in my Algebra classroom in many different ways. Sometimes, I put this technology in the hands of students and sometimes I use it myself to streamline a process.|title=Response: Ways to Use Tech to Differentiate Instruction|date=2018-06-22|author=Larry Ferlazzo|quotee=Sarah Shartzer}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|intransitive|often in the|_|passive|biology}} To ([[cause]] to) [[go through]] a process of [[development]] called [[differentiation]]; to make or become [[different]] in [[form]] or [[function]].
#: {{syn|en|specialize}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=[[S:Insects, Their Ways and Means of Living|Insects: Their Ways and Means of Living]]|year=1930|author=Robert Evans Snodgrass|passage=In Chapter IV we learned that every animal consists of a body, or soma, formed of cells that are '''differentiated''' from the germ cells usually at an early stage of development.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013|author=Lauralee Sherwood|chapter=The Peripheral Endocrine Glands|title=Human Physiology: From Cells to Systems|edition=8th|location=Belmont, CA|publisher=w:Brooks/Cole|isbn=1133108938|page={{gbooks|CZkJAAAAQBAJ|pgT=756|altpg=723|q=differentiate}}|passage=Osteoblasts are derived from stromal cells, a type of connective tissue cell in the bone marrow, whereas osteoclasts '''differentiate''' from macrophages, which are tissue-bound derivatives of monocytes, a type of white blood cell (see p. 402).}}

